d on `canViewTranscript`, `transcriptionEnabled`, and empty timeline creates a rendering issue if `canViewTranscript` is false, transcription is disabled, or the timeline segments are empty. When `chat.reactionHistoryAvailable` is `false`, reactions might not be displayed in `liveTextRows()` as intended in `renderMessages()` because it has a separate check. It is important to confirm whether the `reactionHistory` is cleared when it becomes unavailable. The current implementation in `ConversationPanelViewController.swift` line 219 calls `requestContentRender(transcript: false)` and checks the `chat.$reactionHistoryAvailable` flag, which appears to be a bug, needing to check the reaction availability for the transcript.\n\n\nThe `renderMode()` call, which triggers a transcript render, follows a line that explicitly sets transcript rendering to false, potentially creating a conflict in update logic. Examining desktop behavior under specific conditions, specifically when the transcript is empty but reactions exist, reveals a UI inconsistency; an empty-transcript placeholder message displays alongside the rendered reactions. This contradicts the intended behavior on both platforms because `liveTextRows()` should still be rendered.\n\n\nThe `else` branch in the transcript rendering logic resets text attributes, potentially leading to incorrect styling when switching between attributed and plain text. Specifically, when `transcript.attributedText` is used (with reactions/segments) and then reverts to plain `transcript.text`, the `UITextView` might inherit the style of the last character from `attributedText`. This incorrect attribute inheritance can result in rendering issues, especially when the last row in `liveTranscript()` is a reaction with styles like `.caption` font and `.lightGray` color, because the subsequent text set with `transcript.text` would improperly render with the last style in effect, rather than default attributes.\n\n\nThe code's read-state semantics 